SEC. 51P-350.105. LANDSCAPING.

(a) Landscape plan. All landscaping must be provided as shown on the landscape plan (Exhibit 350B). Unless otherwise stated below, all landscaping must be installed in accordance with Article X. All plant material must be maintained in a healthy, growing condition at all times. A survey of existing trees on the Property with a caliper of more than four inches is included as part of the landscape plan.

(b) Tree Preservation.

(1) Purpose. The purpose of this provision is to:

(A) preserve and enhance the natural beauty, environment, and vegetative space of the Property;

(B) promote and protect the health, safety, and welfare of the public;

(C) provide physical and psychological buffering between the Property and surrounding properties;

(D) safeguard and enhance property values and to protect public and private investment;

(E) conserve energy; and

(F) provide habitat for wildlife.

(2) Definitions.

(A) CALIPER means the diameter of a tree trunk measured six inches above ground level for trees with a caliper of four inches or less. The caliper is measured twelve inches above ground if the caliper at six inches above ground level exceeds four inches. If a tree is of the multi-trunk variety, the caliper of the tree is the diameter of the largest trunk plus half of the diameters of the other trunks.

(B) PROTECTED TREE means a tree that has a caliper of eight inches or more.

(C) REMOVE means an act that causes a tree to decline and die within three years of the following acts or events: cutting down of a tree; compaction of soil above the root system of a tree; changing the natural grade above the root system of the tree; injury to a tree from fire, wind, flood, pests, disease, or other natural causes; application of herbicide or other lethal chemicals; or placement of nonpermeable pavement over the root system of a tree.

(3) Removal of protected trees. Protected trees may not be removed from the Property, unless the tree:

(A) was dead;

(B) had a disease or injury that threatened the life of the tree;

(C) was in danger of falling or had partially fallen;

(D) was in a visibility triangle;

(E) interferes with service provided by a city department or a public utility within a public right-of-way;

(F) threatens public health or safety as determined by the building official;

(G) interferes with maintenance of the water supply, treatment and distribution system, wastewater collection and treatment system, storm water, drainage and flood control facilities, or roadway facilities within the public right of way; or

(H) was on property offered as right-of-way in an approved plat filed of record in the county clerk's office.

(4) Replacement trees.

(A) If a protected tree is removed, a replacement tree must be planted on the Property.

(B) Replacement trees must be planted in soil that meets the requirements of Section 51A-10.106(d).

(C) A replacement tree may not be planted within a visibility triangle, a water course, an existing or proposed street or alley, a utility easement, a driveway, or a sidewalk.

(D) A replacement tree must be a minimum of three inches in caliper and 10 feet in height when planted.

(E) A replacement tree must be located within 100 feet of a verifiable water supply. The watering method must be adequate to maintain the replacement tree in a healthy, growing condition.

(F) Any replacement tree must be planted within 30 days after the removal of the protected tree.

(G) Replacement trees must be maintained in a healthy, growing condition at all times.

(H) Any replacement tree that dies within one year of planting must be replaced with another replacement tree that equals the replacement tree originally required. (Ord. Nos. 21998; 25508)
